      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-6107",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/problem",
          "value": "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{R} \\left(x^2-y^2 +3\\right)  \\textrm d A,$  where $R= [-1,3] \\times [2, 4].$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054242/example-6107.json",
          "file_sha256": "e1532b3a549e7c56c071d96bf340e70c312f4941343334d2dddae46729b2c0e5"
        }
      ],
      "basis": 1795,
      "reason": "Observed MA content supersedes the stored value",
      "action": "replace",
      "transaction_receipt": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054242/edb-import/commit.edn",
      "basis_after": 1796
    },
    {
      "question": "e-6107",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "55052dfb7468af713fd7b2d8d088eaa6a9bdc749a0c1946a627f16b1c899b8f2",
      "new_sha256": "5d11311d97102c638e289c1afff2168c06c62b9aa70e9bcc351ea943eb5dbfdb",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-6107",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "Using the linearity property of double integrals, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{R}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+3)\\text{\u00a0}\\text{d}A & ={\u222c}_{R}{x}^{2}\\text{\u00a0}\\text{d}A-{\u222c}_{R}{y}^{2}\\text{\u00a0}\\text{d}A+{\u222c}_{R}3\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's evaluate each of the three integrals.\n\n- The function under the first integral is independent of $y.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{R}{x}^{2}\\text{\u00a0}\\text{d}A & =(4-2){\\int }_{-1}^{3}{x}^{2}\\text{\u00a0}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}{|}_{-1}^{3} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{27}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{56}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function under the second integral is independent of $x.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{R}{y}^{2}\\text{\u00a0}\\text{d}A & =(3-(-1)){\\int }_{2}^{4}{y}^{2}\\text{\u00a0}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{2}^{4}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}{|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{4}^{3}-\\frac{4}{3}{2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{224}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function under the third integral is a constant, so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{R}3\\,\\text{d}A=3\\,\\text{Area}(R)=3\\cdot (2\\cdot 4)=24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, combining our results, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{R}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+3)\\,\\text{d}A=\\frac{56}{3}-\\frac{224}{3}+24=-32.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054242/example-6107.json",
          "file_sha256": "e1532b3a549e7c56c071d96bf340e70c312f4941343334d2dddae46729b2c0e5"
        }
      ],
