    {
      "question": "e-6108",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "aef4c14b298725f35271d09bbe32d2fafe61facbd23b1052fe38202406076026",
      "new_sha256": "7832a0dc70f918f865e9284f8d0f15eb89b9da1e0f2f7194985958aa4f7d9790",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-6108",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "First, notice that we have $R = R_1 \\cup R_2,$ where $R_1= (0,2) \\times (-1,0)$ and $R_2= (0,2) \\times (0,1).$ So, we can write\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\iint\\limits_{R}  f(x,y)\\ \\textrm d A &= \\iint\\limits_{R_1} 2 \\ \\textrm d A + \\iint\\limits_{R_2}    \\dfrac{1}{xy+x+y+1} \\ \\textrm d A\\[5pt]\n&= 2\\iint\\limits_{R_1} \\textrm d A\n+ \\iint\\limits_{R_2}  \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)(y+1)} \\ \\textrm d A\\[5pt]\n&= 2(2-0)(0-(-1)) + \\Bigg( \\int_0^2 \\dfrac{1}{x+1} \\ \\textrm d x \\Bigg) \\Bigg( \\int_0^1 \\dfrac{1}{y+1}\\ \\textrm d y \\Bigg) \\[5pt]\n&= 4 + \\Big[\\ln(x+1)\\Big]_0^2  \\cdot \\Big[\\ln(y+1)\\Big]_0^1\\[5pt] \n&= 4+ \\ln 3\\ln 2.\n}\n$$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14054242/example-6108.json",
          "file_sha256": "b0b1d742a6c3a79e660b743f8779736a296acb7524f0263fdd018662d9b478f1"
